ural organic healthy food side of  things which is we see growing double  digits as a market so Walter let's talk  about those headwinds a little bit I  think it's true I read you'll correct me  if I'm wrong that your same source sales  are actually down and your margins are  down does that make it more difficult  for you to pivot actually the the mark  the decrease in the margins is our pivot  we basically said over the next couple  years we're going to take the gross  margins down incrementally quarter to  quarter in conjunction with our cost  restructuring efforts which were well  underway in doing so the  strategic pivot is around reshaping the  company transforming the company to  compete better in the marketplace today  at the same time as continuing to launch  new digital platforms and new ways of  reaching our customers seeing that the  consumer could have less disposable  income in his or her wallet do we run  the risk that this positive trend we  have been seeing away from GMOs to more  natural organic foods could we lose that  while people are looking to cut personal  spending I don't think so because I  think people's personal health and  vitality Trump's that and I think we've  seen through many ups and downs over the  years that in the end people prioritize  food and the things that they're eating  at a higher level what you do see  sometimes they'll shift from the amount  of food spent out of the home to food  spent in the home but I think that  generally speaking this trend towards a  healthier Whole Foods is going to  continue unabated Walter let me take you  back moment to what Stephanie was  talking about these 365 stores who are  you hoping to take market share from  with those well there's an you see the  marketplace today there's a number of  smaller store competitors out there that  are taking that are doing a nice job per  ticking up serving customers you know  smaller sized or smaller sorts of offers  and we see we don't see any reason why  